course: everyday
lesson: 502
title: Minding the little ones: «you're the oldest»
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «You're the oldest» is something lots of big brothers and sisters hear. In Birmingham, Ifeoma, 10, is the oldest of five; Mum and Dad run a small shop. «Watch the twins while I nip to the shop,» says Mum. Two minutes later the twins have drawn a rainbow on the wall. Mum comes back: «Ifeoma! I told you to watch them!» Ifeoma is furious: she didn't do it. Older kids often get blamed for what the little ones do, and that's unfair when they are children too. She can say calmly, «I was watching, they were too fast,» and it's fair to ask not to be in charge every time.
explain: Helping is good, but some kids carry too much. In Peterborough, Mum works evenings, so Kacper, 9, gives his brothers, 4 and 6, their tea and puts them to bed most nights. He does his homework at ten and is tired and grumpy at school. Kids who do a lot of looking after are called young carers, and there are groups and help for them and their families: Kacper should tell a teacher. In Derry, Mairead's mam says, «You're a great wee help» («wee» = little), and Mairead glows. Then she misses her mate's party because she has to mind the boys, and she's gutted (very disappointed). Missing out can be unfair: tell your mam it matters, so someone else might mind them next time.
explain: The oldest's things need protecting too. In Houston, Aaliyah's little sisters wreck her slime collection. Mom says, «They're little, let it go.» Aaliyah yells, «It's always my fault or my problem!» A lock box or a high shelf is a fair ask. In London, Harrison's little brothers break his PlayStation controller and Dad says, «You should've put it away.» «That's peak,» mutters Harrison (London slang for «so unfair, so bad»). It's fair to ask that the little ones say sorry too. In Leicester, every time there's a fight Aarav hears, «You're the oldest, you should know better.» Fairness means everyone takes their share: «Can we each say what happened?»
explain: Little ones copy you because you're their hero. In Glasgow, Kenzie's wee brother, 3, follows her everywhere: «Kenzie dae it, me dae it!» («dae» = do). Her pals say, «Aw, he's pure cute» («pure» = really). Kenzie says, «Try livin' wi' him,» but secretly she loves it. In Galway, Nuala changes her baby brother's nappy while Mam's in the shower. «Rank!» she gags (Irish slang for disgusting), then laughs: little jobs help families, and you can say when it's too much.
explain: When something goes wrong, small things you can fix; big things need a grown-up. In Nashville, Shelby takes her little brothers to the park on Saturday; one falls off the slide and cuts his knee. «I'm fixin' to cry,» she thinks («fixin' to» is Southern US for «about to»). She takes a breath, puts on a band-aid (a plaster) and hugs him. Well done, and anything bigger needs a grown-up straight away. In Leeds, Tomasz's little sister cries and wants Mum, and he doesn't know what to do. He rings Mum, who talks to her on speaker. You don't have to solve everything: calling a grown-up is smart.
fact: Older children often get blamed for what the little ones do, which is unfair when they are children too.
fact: It is fair for an older child to explain calmly what happened and to ask not to be in charge every time.
fact: Children who do a lot of looking after at home are called young carers, and there is help for them and their families.
fact: If minding siblings leaves you too tired for school or homework, tell a teacher.
fact: Helping is good, but always missing out on your own life is unfair; you can tell your parents it matters.
fact: The oldest child's things deserve protecting too; a lock box or high shelf is a fair ask.
fact: Fairness means each child takes their share of the blame, not always the oldest.
fact: Little siblings copy older ones because they look up to them.
fact: A child can deal with small hurts like a cut knee, but anything serious needs a grown-up straight away.
fact: When you are stuck, calling a grown-up is the smart thing to do.
fact: «Wee» means little, «gutted» means very disappointed, «peak» is London slang for very unfair or bad, «dae» is Scots for do, «rank» means disgusting, «fixin' to» means about to, and a «band-aid» is a plaster.
exercise: The twins draw on the wall while Ifeoma is watching them. What can she say calmly? | «I was watching, they were too fast» | «It's your fault for going out, Mum» | «I drew it, sorry, I'll never do it again» | «Ask the twins, I wasn't even there»
exercise: Why is it unfair when Ifeoma gets blamed? | She is a child too, and the twins did it | She was out at the shop when it happened | She is the youngest one in the family | It's not unfair; the oldest is always to blame
exercise: Kacper puts his brothers to bed most nights and does homework at ten. What is he? | A young carer | A babysitter | A night worker | Lazy at school
exercise: Who should Kacper tell? | A teacher | Nobody | His brothers | The shop
exercise: Mairead misses her mate's party to mind her brothers. How does she feel? | Gutted | Proud | Relieved | Bored
exercise: What can Mairead do about it? | Tell her mam it matters | Never help again | Take the boys to the party | Leave them alone at home
exercise: Aaliyah's sisters keep wrecking her slime. What is a fair ask? | A lock box or a high shelf | That her sisters move out of the house | That she gets to wreck their toys back | Nothing, the oldest should just let it go
exercise: Harrison's brothers break his controller and Dad blames Harrison. What is fair to ask? | That the little ones say sorry too | That Dad buy him three controllers | That his brothers never play again | That Harrison gets their pocket money
exercise: «That's peak,» says Harrison. What does «peak» mean in London slang? | So unfair, so bad | At the top | Amazing | Quiet
exercise: Aarav always hears «you should know better». What can he ask? | «Can we each say what happened?» | «Can I be the youngest from now on?» | «Can I punish them myself next time?» | «Can I go and live at Nani's house?»
exercise: Why does Kenzie's wee brother copy her? | He looks up to her | He wants to annoy her | Mum told him to | He's bored
exercise: «Kenzie dae it, me dae it.» What does «dae» mean? | Do | Day | Die | Don't
exercise: Nuala changes the nappy and says «Rank!» What does she mean? | It's disgusting | It's brilliant | It's her turn | It's in order
exercise: Shelby's brother cuts his knee at the park. What did she do? | Put on a band-aid and hugged him | Left him there and ran home alone | Called an ambulance for the scratch | Told him to stop crying and get up
exercise: What should Shelby do if something bigger happens? | Get a grown-up straight away | Sort it all out herself | Wait and see if it's fine | Post about it online first
exercise: Tomasz's sister cries and wants Mum. What does Tomasz do? | Rings Mum, who talks to her on speaker | Shouts at her to stop it right now | Locks himself in his room till Mum's back | Gives her sweets till she's sick of them
exercise: «I'm fixin' to cry.» What does «fixin' to» mean? | About to | Mending | Trying not to | Never going to
test: «you're the oldest so it's ALWAYS your fault, end of». What does the lesson say? | Everyone takes their share; it's unfair to blame only the oldest | That's right, the oldest is in charge of everything that happens | Only the youngest can ever be to blame for anything at home | The parents are to blame for every single thing that goes wrong
test: What would probably happen if Kacper kept looking after his brothers every night and told no one? | He'd stay tired and his school would suffer | He'd get more rested as time went on | His homework would get easier and easier | Nothing at all would change for him
test: «Aaliyah's being dramatic, it's just slime». What's the fair view? | Her things deserve protecting too | Older kids shouldn't have toys | Slime doesn't count as a thing | She should give the slime away
test: Kenzie's pal says, «he's pure cute». What does «pure» mean here? | Really | Clean | Only | Not
test: «Nuala's mam made her change a nappy, that's child labour». What's the fair view? | Little jobs help, and she can say when it's too much | It's against the law for any kid to help at home | She should refuse to help with anything ever again | Babies should change their own nappies by age one
test: A cartoon babysitter kid fixes a broken arm with tape and a lolly. What should really happen? | Get a grown-up straight away | Tape works fine for arms | Just give two lollies | Wait till tea time
test: «Tomasz rang his mum, what a baby, he should sort it himself». What does the lesson say? | Calling a grown-up when you're stuck is smart | A big brother must solve everything alone | He should have made his sister stop on her own | Phoning your mum means you've failed the job
test: ★★★ Ifeoma watches the twins from 4:50 to 5:20, Mum's 15 minutes late back. If Mum had been on time, how long would Ifeoma have watched them? | 15 minutes | 30 minutes | 45 minutes | 35 minutes
test: ★★★ Kacper puts his 2 brothers to bed 5 nights a week, and reads each of them one story a night. Mum does Saturdays and Sundays. How many stories does Kacper read in 2 weeks? | 20 | 28 | 10 | 14
test: «Mairead's mam called her a great wee help, so she should always mind the boys». What's missing? | Missing out can be unfair; she can say it matters | Being praised means she must always say yes | She should be paid by her mam every time | Nothing; praise means it's always fair
test: «the little ones did it but Dad said Harrison should've put it away, so it's Harrison's fault». Who should say sorry? | The little ones too | Only Harrison | Only Dad | Nobody at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attribution of blame to the eldest child for acts committed by younger siblings? | It is unfair, the eldest also being a child | It is just, the eldest being in sole charge of the household | It is required by the customs of every family | It is appropriate whenever the parents are absent
exam: State the designation applied to a child who habitually prepares meals for and attends to younger siblings in the absence of a parent. | A young carer | A minor domestic | A junior guardian | A household warden
exam: Identify the course of action recommended where a child in charge of a sibling encounters an injury more serious than a minor cut. | The immediate summoning of an adult | Treatment of the injury by the child unaided | Waiting to ascertain whether it improves by evening | Concealment of the injury from the parents
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the imitation of an elder sibling by a younger one. | It arises because the younger child admires the elder | It arises solely from a wish to vex the elder sibling | It is directed by the parents as a form of discipline | It indicates that the younger child is unwell
